sec.p:
To uncover task-related neural dynamics in the data, we implemented a novel variant of Targeted Dimensionality Reduction (TDR) (Mante et al., 2013; Aoi et al., 2020). Specifically, we modelled activity of neuron i at time t on trial k as
ri(k)(t)=w1,i(t)f(θ(k))+w2,i(t)g(θ(k))+w0,i(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle r_{i}^{(k)}(t)=w_{1, i}(t) f\left(\theta^{(k)}\right)+w_{2, i}(t) g\left(\theta^{(k)}\right)+w_{0, i}(t)$$\end{document}
where θ(k) was the stimulus presented on trial k. The stimulus-dependent factors f(θ), g(θ) and temporal factors wp,i(t) were fitted directly to the observed neural responses using least squares regression (Methods). The resulting model offered a compact description of the neural population responses in terms of a set of stimulus-dependent factors shared across neurons and temporal factors shared across stimuli.

We considered continuous-time recurrent linear networks driven by noisy inputs:


dxidt=∑j=1NAijxj+ui(s,t)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{d x_{i}}{d t}=\sum_{j=1}^{N} A_{i j} x_{j}+u_{i}(s, t)$$\end{document}

We minimised the following loss function:
L=∫0tdl(t)L(t)dt+βE \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathcal{L}=\int_{0}^{t_{d}} l(t) L(t) d t+\beta E$$\end{document}
where the first term quantifies the decoder performance L(t) = Σ i>j 1/SNR(si, sj, t) weighted over delays by l(t) and the second term quantifies the energy of responses weighted by the regularisation parameter β. The weighting function was set as l(t) = δ(t − td) (where td is the decision time), with the exception of Supplementary Figure S4 where we set l(t) = 1 for 0 ≤ t ≤ td and 0 otherwise. We set β = 10−6 for Figures 1 and S3 and β = 0 otherwise. For delta input tasks we set td = 50, and for finite cue tasks, we set Tcue = 1, td = 10 (with the exception of Supplementary Figure S7, where Tcue was systematically varied).

The Legendre Memory Unit is governed by the equations
τdxidt=∑j=1NAijxj+biu(t)y(t)=∑i=1Nwixi(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \tau \frac{d x_{i}}{d t} & =\sum_{j=1}^{N} A_{i j} x_{j}+b_{i} u(t) \\ y(t) & =\sum_{i=1}^{N} w_{i} x_{i}(t),\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where u(t) is a scalar input time series, τ is a global network time constant, Aij are the recurrent network weights, bi is an input loading vector and wi are the readout weights (Voelker et al., 2019; Voelker, 2019). Classic results from control theory provide methods to optimise the parameters of the above system such that the output y(t) is related to the input u(t) by a desired transfer function f, i.e. y ≈ (u ⋆ f)(t) where ⋆ is convolution (Partington, 2004; Brockett, 2015). Voelker (2019) considered the case of a delay transfer function y(t)≈(u⋆δ−t′)(t)=u(t−t′)\begin{document}$y(t) \approx\left(u \star \delta_{-t^{\prime}}\right)(t)=u\left(t-t^{\prime}\right)$\end{document}, which we show in the Supplementary Material is a critical component of the Bayesian ideal observer solution to the workingmemory task we consider (Supplementary Figure S2). In approximating the delay transfer function using the above SSM, it was further shown that the solution can be understood through the lens of function approximation theory - each element of the network state xi(t) in the optimised system computes the coefficient of a Legendre polynomial approximation to the input time series u(t′) over an interval t − τ ≤ t′≤ t, and subsequent work has shown how this approach can be extended to a wide range of approximating function classes (Gu et al., 2020, 2023). As a consequence, it is possible to reconstruct arbitrary functions of the input time series over this interval through an appropriate choice of readout weights wi, as described below.

The parameters of the LMU are found by forming a Padé approximation of the desired transfer function in the Laplace domain (Partington, 2004; Brockett, 2015; Voelker et al., 2019). The resulting system can be written in multiple equivalent bases, all of which implement an identical transfer function. In the standard Legendre basis of the LMU, the recurrent weight matrix is given by:
Aij=2i{−1i<j(−1)(i−j+1)i≥j \begin{document}$$\displaystyle A_{i j}=2 i \begin{cases}-1 & i<j \\ (-1)^{(i-j+1)} & i \geq j\end{cases}$$\end{document}
and the input vector is given by bi = (2i)(−1)i−1. In this basis, the activations xi(t) represent the delayed inputs as u(t−t′)≈∑i=1NPi−1(t′τ)xi(t)=p(t′)⋅x(t)\begin{document}$u\left(t-t^{\prime}\right) \approx \sum_{i=1}^{N} \mathcal{P}_{i-1}\left(\frac{t^{\prime}}{\tau}\right) x_{i}(t)=\mathbf{p}\left(t^{\prime}\right) \cdot \mathbf{x}(t)$\end{document} where Pi(t′τ)\begin{document}$\mathcal{P}_{i}\left(\frac{t^{\prime}}{\tau}\right)$\end{document} is the ith shifted Legendre polynomial defined on the interval 0≤t′τ≤1\begin{document}$0 \leq \frac{t^{\prime}}{\tau} \leq 1$\end{document} (Voelker et al., 2019). As a consequence, an arbitrary filtering of the input time series can be approximately recovered by setting the readout weights as ∫0τf(t′)u(t−t′)dt′≈∫0τf(t′)p(t′)⋅x(t)dt′=w⋅x(t)\begin{document}$\int_{0}^{\tau} f\left(t^{\prime}\right) u\left(t-t^{\prime}\right) d t^{\prime} \approx \int_{0}^{\tau} f\left(t^{\prime}\right) \mathbf{p}\left(t^{\prime}\right) \cdot \mathbf{x}(t) d t^{\prime}=\mathbf{w} \cdot \mathbf{x}(t)$\end{document} where w=∫0τf(t′)p(t′)dt′\begin{document}$\mathbf{w}=\int_{0}^{\tau} f\left(t^{\prime}\right) \mathbf{p}\left(t^{\prime}\right) d t^{\prime}$\end{document}.

We fitted the targeted dimensionality reduction (TDR) model
ri(k)(t)=∑p=0Pwp,i(t)fp(θ(k)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle r_{i}^{(k)}(t)=\sum_{p=0}^{P} w_{p, i}(t) f_{p}\left(\theta^{(k)}\right)$$\end{document}
to the mean-subtracted spike counts ri(k)(t)\begin{document}$r_{i}^{(k)}(t)$\end{document} using alternating least squares, iteratively solving for wp,I and fp until convergence. We set f0(θ) = 1 for the stimulus-independent subspace. In this notation, f1(θ) = f(θ), f2(θ) = g(θ), f0(θ) = 1 recovers the P = 2 model described in the main text. Given the 8 stimuli in the experimental task, the fp(θ) parameters were given by 8-dimensional vectors fp = [fp(θ1), …, fp(θ8)]⊺ that were inferred directly from the data. We initialised fp(θ) ∼ N(0, 1) (for p > 0) and began the optimisation algorithm by updating wp,i(t). For Nstim = 8 stimuli, T time points and N neurons in a given experimental session, the model had (P + 1)NT + PNstim parameters, with NT parameters for each of the wp,i(t) components and Nstim parameters for each of the fp components. In contrast, the PSTHs of the data contained NstimNT datapoints. This reduction in parameters reflects the shared structure of the TDR model across stimuli, which incorporates the assumption that the population PSTHs for different stimuli should have a similar temporal profile.

For cross-validation, we split the data into two subsets of randomly selected trials of equal size. The TDR model was fit to each split and used to predict the remaining split. We reported the cross-validated coefficient of determination
R2=1−∑t=1,i=1,θ=1t=T,i=N,θ=NStim(r^i(θ,t)−r¯i(θ,t))2∑t=1,i=1,θ=1t=T,i=N,θ=NStim(r¯i(θ,t)−r¯total)2 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle R^{2}=1-\frac{\sum_{t=1, i=1, \theta=1}^{t=T, i=N, \theta=N_{\text {Stim }}}\left(\hat{r}_{i}(\theta, t)-\bar{r}_{i}(\theta, t)\right)^{2}}{\sum_{t=1, i=1, \theta=1}^{t=T, i=N, \theta=N_{\text {Stim }}}\left(\bar{r}_{i}(\theta, t)-\bar{r}_{\text {total }}\right)^{2}}$$\end{document}
where r^i(θ,t)=∑p=0Pwp,i(t)fp(θ)\begin{document}$\hat{r}_{i}(\theta, t)=\sum_{p=0}^{P} w_{p, i}(t) f_{p}(\theta)$\end{document} is the predicted PSTH formed by the TDR fit to the train-ing data, r¯i(θ,t)=1NTestTrials(θ)∑k∈TestTrials(θ)ri(k)(t)\begin{document}$\bar{r}_{i}(\theta, t)=\frac{1}{N_{\text {TestTrials }}(\theta)} \sum_{k \in \operatorname{TestTrials}(\theta)} r_{i}^{(k)}(t)$\end{document} is the PSTH formed on the test data and r¯total=1TNNStim∑t=1,i=1,θ=1t=T,i=N,θ=NStimr¯i(θ,t)\begin{document}$\bar{r}_{\text {total }}=\frac{1}{T N N_{\text {Stim }}} \sum_{t=1, i=1, \theta=1}^{t=T, i=N, \theta=N_{\text {Stim }}} \bar{r}_{i}(\theta, t)$\end{document}. Given that the TDR model was underparameterised, we also reported this measure without cross-validation (where r^,r¯,r¯total\begin{document}$\hat{r}, \bar{r}, \bar{r}_{\text {total }}$\end{document} were formed on the full dataset). We repeated this cross-validation for 25 random two-way splits of the data (with random TDR parameter initialisations) and averaged R2 both over splits within each recording session (grey traces in Figure 5B) and over recording sessions (red bars in Figure 5B). We compared this to a PSTH only model in which the predicted PSTH was r^i(θ,t)=1NTrainTrials(θ)∑k∈TrainTrials(θ)ri(k)(t)\begin{document}$\hat{r}_{i}(\theta, t)=\frac{1}{N_{\text {TrainTrials }}(\theta)} \sum_{k \in \operatorname{TrainTrials}(\theta)} r_{i}^{(k)}(t)$\end{document}.Note that a TDR model with P = Nstim and w0,i(t) = 0 is equivalent to this PSTH only model.

Given the initial condition x0 = x(t0), Equation 1 has the general solution for t ≥ t0
(3)
x(t)=eA(t−t0)x0+∫t0teA(t−τ)u(s,τ)dτ. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A\left(t-t_{0}\right)} \mathbf{x}_{0}+\int_{t_{0}}^{t} e^{A(t-\tau)} \mathbf{u}(s, \tau) d \tau . \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Inserting Equation 2 into Equation 3 gives (for t, ts ≥ t0)
x(t)=eA(t−t0)x0+eA(t−ts)u(s)Θ(t−ts)+∫t0teA(t−τ)n(τ)dτ, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A\left(t-t_{0}\right)} \mathbf{x}_{0}+e^{A\left(t-t_{s}\right)} \mathbf{u}(s) \Theta\left(t-t_{s}\right)+\int_{t_{0}}^{t} e^{A(t-\tau)} \mathbf{n}(\tau) d \tau$$\end{document}
where Θ(t) is the Heaviside step function. Using the shorthand for the stimulus-conditioned and marginal means of a variable a as a―(s):=∫−∞∞p(a∣s)ada\begin{document}$\overline{\mathbf{a}}(s):=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} p(\mathbf{a} \mid s) \mathbf{a} d \mathbf{a}$\end{document} and a―:=∫−∞∞p(a)ada\begin{document}$\overline{\mathbf{a}}:=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} p(\mathbf{a}) \mathbf{a} d \mathbf{a}$\end{document}, the stimulus-conditioned mean and covariance of x(t) are
(4)
x―(s,t)=eA(t−t0)x―0+eA(t−ts)u(s)Θ(t−ts), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \overline{\mathbf{x}}(s, t)=e^{A\left(t-t_{0}\right)} \overline{\mathbf{x}}_{0}+e^{A\left(t-t_{s}\right)} \mathbf{u}(s) \Theta\left(t-t_{s}\right), \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and
(5)
Σx(t)=⟨(x(t)−x―(s,t))(x(t)−x―(s,t))⊤⟩=eA(t−t0)Σx0eA⊤(t−t0)+∫t0teA(t−τ)ΣneA⊤(t−τ)dτ=eA(t−t0)Σx0eA⊤(t−t0)⏟Σx(t)IC+∫0t−t0eAτΣneA⊤τdτ⏟Σx(t)input, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)} & =\left\langle(\mathbf{x}(t)-\overline{\mathbf{x}}(s, t))(\mathbf{x}(t)-\overline{\mathbf{x}}(s, t))^{\top}\right\rangle \\ & =e^{A\left(t-t_{0}\right)} \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}_{0}} e^{A^{\top}\left(t-t_{0}\right)}+\int_{t_{0}}^{t} e^{A(t-\tau)} \Sigma_{\mathbf{n}} e^{A^{\top}(t-\tau)} d \tau \\ & =\underbrace{e^{A\left(t-t_{0}\right)} \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}_{0}} e^{A^{\top}\left(t-t_{0}\right)}}_{\Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\mathrm{IC}}}+\underbrace{\int_{0}^{t-t_{0}} e^{A \tau} \Sigma_{\mathbf{n}} e^{A^{\top} \tau} d \tau}_{\Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\text {input }}}, \tag{5} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where we assumed that the initial condition is distributed as x0∼N(x―0,Σx0)\begin{document}$\mathbf{x}_{0} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\overline{\mathbf{x}}_{0}, \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}\right)$\end{document} and is independent of the subsequent input noise n ∼ 𝒩(0, Σn). The terms ∑x(t)IC\begin{document}$\sum_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\mathrm{IC}}$\end{document} and ∑x(t)input\begin{document}$\sum_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\text {input }}$\end{document} capture the response covariance generated by propagating the initial state and subsequent noise input through the recurrent dynamics, respectively. The response at time t under each stimulus is normally distributed over trials, with x(t)∼N(x―(s,t),Σx(t))\begin{document}$\mathbf{x}(t) \sim N\left(\overline{\mathbf{x}}(s, t), \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}\right)$\end{document}, and the covariance Σx(t) is independent of the stimulus s.

We consider the case where one of M possible input stimuli s ∈ {0, …,M −1} is presented, and optimised the performance of an optimal decoder of s from the network response x(t). Since the response statistics are Gaussian with stimulus-independent covariance, the performance of an optimal decoder when M = 2 is given by a linear readout ŝ = Θ(wLD · x(t) + c), where wLD∝Σx(t)−1(x―(s=1,t)−x―(s=0,t))=Σx(t)−1Δx―(t)\begin{document}$\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{LD}} \propto \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{-1}(\overline{\mathbf{x}}(s=1, t)-\overline{\mathbf{x}}(s=0, t))=\Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{-1} \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t)$\end{document} is the linear discriminant vector and c is a bias term (Chadwick et al., 2023). The performance of an arbitrary linear decoder w can be described by a signal-to-noise ratio given by:
(6)
SNR(w,t):=(w⋅Δx―(t))2w⋅Σx(t)w≤SNR(t):=SNR(wLD,t)=Δx―(t)⋅Σx(t)−1Δx―(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{SNR}(\mathbf{w}, t):=\frac{(\mathbf{w} \cdot \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t))^{2}}{\mathbf{w} \cdot \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)} \mathbf{w}} \leq \operatorname{SNR}(t):=\operatorname{SNR}\left(\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{LD}}, t\right)=\Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t) \cdot \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{-1} \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t) \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The derivative of this loss function with respect to the weights is
∂L∂Aij=∂L∂Σx(t)⋅∂Σx(t)∂Aij+∂L∂Δx―(t)⋅∂Δx―(t)∂Aij=−1(Δx―⊤(t)Σx(t)−1Δx―(t))2(∂Δx―⊤(t)Σx(t)−1Δx―(t)∂Σx(t)⋅∂Σx(t)∂Aij+∂Δx―⊤(t)Σx(t)−1Δx―(t)∂Δx―(t)⋅∂Δx―(t)∂Aij), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\partial L}{\partial A_{i j}} & =\frac{\partial L}{\partial \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}} \cdot \frac{\partial \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}}{\partial A_{i j}}+\frac{\partial L}{\partial \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t)} \cdot \frac{\partial \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t)}{\partial A_{i j}} \\ & =-\frac{1}{\left(\Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}^{\top}(t) \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{-1} \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t)\right)^{2}}\left(\frac{\partial \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}^{\top}(t) \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{-1} \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t)}{\partial \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}} \cdot \frac{\partial \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}}{\partial A_{i j}}+\frac{\partial \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}^{\top}(t) \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{-1} \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t)}{\partial \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t)} \cdot \frac{\partial \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t)}{\partial A_{i j}}\right),\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where M · N = Trace(MN⊺) = Σi,j MijNij denotes a sum over elements. Given the derivatives ∂Δx―(t)∂Aij\begin{document}$\frac{\partial \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t)}{\partial A_{i j}}$\end{document} and ∂Σx(t)∂Aij\begin{document}$\frac{\partial \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}}{\partial A_{i j}}$\end{document}, the above equation allows for a direct computation of the gradient of the loss function with respect to A. We describe how these derivatives can be computed in Section C.

For the general setting of networks with N neurons, M stimuli, and a penalty on response energy, we optimised the loss
L=∫0∞l(t)L(t)dt+βE, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathcal{L}=\int_{0}^{\infty} l(t) L(t) d t+\beta E$$\end{document}
where L(t)=∑0≤s<s′≤M−1[(x―(s,t)−x―(s′,t))⊤Σx(t)−1(x―(s,t)−x―(s′,t))]−1\begin{document}$L(t)=\sum_{0 \leq s<s^{\prime} \leq M-1}\left[\left(\overline{\mathbf{x}}(s, t)-\overline{\mathbf{x}}\left(s^{\prime}, t\right)\right)^{\top} \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{-1}\left(\overline{\mathbf{x}}(s, t)-\overline{\mathbf{x}}\left(s^{\prime}, t\right)\right)\right]^{-1}$\end{document} and E=∑s=0M−1∫0∞‖x―(s,t)‖2dt\begin{document}$E=\sum_{s=0}^{M-1} \int_{0}^{\infty}\|\overline{\mathbf{x}}(s, t)\|^{2} d t$\end{document}.

The derivative of the mean response (Equation 4) is


dx―(s,t)dAij=deA(t−t0)dAijx―0+deA(t−ts)dAiju(s)Θ(t−ts).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{d \overline{\mathbf{x}}(s, t)}{d A_{i j}}=\frac{d e^{A\left(t-t_{0}\right)}}{d A_{i j}} \overline{\mathbf{x}}_{0}+\frac{d e^{A\left(t-t_{s}\right)}}{d A_{i j}} \mathbf{u}(s) \Theta\left(t-t_{s}\right)$$\end{document}

The loss functions we consider depend on the difference in mean response to the two stimuli Δx―(t)=x―(s=1,t)−x―(s=0,t).\begin{document}$\Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t)=\overline{\mathbf{x}}(s=1, t)-\overline{\mathbf{x}}(s=0, t)$\end{document}, which has derivative:


dΔx―(t)dAij=deA(t−ts)dAijΔuΘ(t−ts)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{d \Delta \overline{\mathbf{x}}(t)}{d A_{i j}}=\frac{d e^{A\left(t-t_{s}\right)}}{d A_{i j}} \Delta \mathbf{u} \Theta\left(t-t_{s}\right)$$\end{document}

The derivative of the covariance (Equation 5) requires the solution of two Lyapunov equations, with numerical solvers widely available. The first Lyapunov equation is the result of integrating ∑x(t)input\begin{document}$\sum_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\text {input }}$\end{document} by parts
∫0t−t0ddτ[eAτΣneA⊤τ]dτ=[eAτΣneA⊤τ]0t−t0⟹AΣx(t)input+Σx(t)inputA⊤+Φ=0, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \int_{0}^{t-t_{0}} \frac{d}{d \tau}\left[e^{A \tau} \Sigma_{\mathbf{n}} e^{A^{\top} \tau}\right] d \tau & =\left[e^{A \tau} \Sigma_{\mathbf{n}} e^{A^{\top} \tau}\right]_{0}^{t-t_{0}} \\ \Longrightarrow A \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\text {input }}+\Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\text {input }} A^{\top}+\Phi & =0,\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where Φ=Σn−eA(t−t0)ΣneA⊤(t−t0)\begin{document}$\Phi=\Sigma_{\mathbf{n}}-e^{A\left(t-t_{0}\right)} \Sigma_{\mathbf{n}} e^{A^{\top}\left(t-t_{0}\right)}$\end{document}. The second Lyapunov equation results from differentiating the first with respect to Aij to obtain
AdΣx(t)inputdAij+dΣx(t)inputdAijA⊤+Ψ=0, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle A \frac{d \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\text {input }}}{d A_{i j}}+\frac{d \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\text {input }}}{d A_{i j}} A^{\top}+\Psi=0$$\end{document}
where Ψ=dΦdAij+E(ij)Σx(t)+Σx(t)E(ji)\begin{document}$\Psi=\frac{d \Phi}{d A_{i j}}+E^{(i j)} \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}+\Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)} E^{(j i)}$\end{document}, with Ekl(ij)=δikδjl\begin{document}$E_{k l}^{(i j)}=\delta_{i k} \delta_{j l}$\end{document} and dΦdAij=−deA(t−t0)dAijΣneA⊤(t−t0)−eA(t−t0)ΣndeA⊤(t−t0)dAij\begin{document}$\frac{d \Phi}{d A_{i j}}=-\frac{d e^{A\left(t-t_{0}\right)}}{d A_{i j}} \Sigma_{\mathbf{n}} e^{A^{\top}\left(t-t_{0}\right)}-e^{A\left(t-t_{0}\right)} \Sigma_{\mathbf{n}} \frac{d e^{A^{\top}\left(t-t_{0}\right)}}{d A_{i j}}$\end{document}. Since this is another Lyapunov equation it can be solved in terms of A and Ψ to obtain d∑x(t)inputdAij\begin{document}$\frac{d \sum_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\text {input }}}{d A_{i j}}$\end{document}. The derivative of the network covariance dΣx(t)dAij=dΣx(t)ICdAij+dΣx(t)inputdAij\begin{document}$\frac{d \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}}{d A_{i j}}=\frac{d \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\mathrm{IC}}}{d A_{i j}}+\frac{d \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\text {input }}}{d A_{i j}}$\end{document} can then be evaluated by handling d∑x(t)inputdAij\begin{document}$\frac{d \sum_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\text {input }}}{d A_{i j}}$\end{document} as described here and dΣx(t)ICdAij\begin{document}$\frac{d \Sigma_{\mathbf{x}(t)}^{\mathrm{IC}}}{d A_{i j}}$\end{document} using a numerical solver for the derivative of the matrix exponential, as described above for the derivative of the mean (assuming that the initial state covariance Σx0\begin{document}$\Sigma_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}$\end{document} is independent of the weights A).

Methods which do not explicitly optimise the probability of correct choice may nevertheless yield very similar learning dynamics to those produced by our method. To illustrate this finding, we derive analytical expressions for the updates generated via backpropagation through time (BPTT) and show that numerical implementation of these updates generates near-identical weight trajectories to those produced by our method. Specifically, we consider BPTT on the continuous-time linear recurrent neural network x˙=Ax+u(s,t)=:f(x,u)\begin{document}$\dot{\mathbf{x}}=A \mathbf{x}+\mathbf{u}(s, t)=: \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u})$\end{document} with u(s, t) = u(s)δ(t − ts) + n(t). On each trial, we consider a trajectory from initial time point t0 to decision time td with a loss L that depends only on the final time point x(td). The continuous-time BPTT updates are then given by
(7)
dLdA=∑i,j=1N∫t0td∂L∂xi(td)∂xi(td)∂xj(t)∂fj(x(t),u(t))∂Adt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d L}{d A}=\sum_{i, j=1}^{N} \int_{t_{0}}^{t_{d}} \frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{i}\left(t_{d}\right)} \frac{\partial x_{i}\left(t_{d}\right)}{\partial x_{j}(t)} \frac{\partial f_{j}(\mathbf{x}(t), \mathbf{u}(t))}{\partial A} d t . \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

We consider the squared error loss function L(td) = (w · x(td) + c − s)2 = (ŝ − s)2. The terms in Equation (7) are the ∂L∂x(td)=2(w⋅x(td)+c−s)w,∂x(td)∂x(t)=eA(td−t),∂fi(x,u)∂Ajk=δijxk\begin{document}$\frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{x}\left(t_{d}\right)}=2\left(\mathbf{w} \cdot \mathbf{x}\left(t_{d}\right)+c-s\right) \mathbf{w}, \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}\left(t_{d}\right)}{\partial \mathbf{x}(t)}=e^{A\left(t_{d}-t\right)}, \frac{\partial f_{i}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u})}{\partial A_{j k}}=\delta_{i j} x_{k}$\end{document}. Therefore


dLdAjk=∑i=1N∫t0td∂L∂xi(td)[eA(td−t)]ijxk(t)dt⟹dLdA=∫t0tdeA⊤(td−t)∂L∂x(td)x⊤(t)dt.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{d L}{d A_{j k}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} \int_{t_{0}}^{t_{d}} \frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{i}\left(t_{d}\right)}\left[e^{A\left(t_{d}-t\right)}\right]_{i j} x_{k}(t) d t \Longrightarrow \frac{d L}{d A}=\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{d}} e^{A^{\top}\left(t_{d}-t\right)} \frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{x}\left(t_{d}\right)} \mathbf{x}^{\top}(t) d t$$\end{document}

We next consider the stationary state limit t0 → −∞, with


dLdA=∫0∞eA⊤t∂L∂x(td)x⊤(td−t)dt.
