A matrix with eigendecomposition A=λ1v1v~1⊤+λ2v2v~2⊤\begin{document}$A=\lambda_{1} \mathbf{v}_{1} \tilde{\mathbf{v}}_{1}^{\top}+\lambda_{2} \mathbf{v}_{2} \tilde{\mathbf{v}}_{2}^{\top}$\end{document} and λ ∈ ℝ can be expressed in Schur form as A=λ1q1q1⊤+λ2q2q2⊤+ωq2q1⊤\begin{document}$A=\lambda_{1} \mathbf{q}_{1} \mathbf{q}_{1}^{\top}+\lambda_{2} \mathbf{q}_{2} \mathbf{q}_{2}^{\top}+\omega \mathbf{q}_{2} \mathbf{q}_{1}^{\top}$\end{document}, where ω is a functionally-feedforward weight from the input Schur mode q1:=v~1/‖v~1‖\begin{document}$\mathbf{q}_{1}:=\tilde{\mathbf{v}}_{1} /\left\|\tilde{\mathbf{v}}_{1}\right\|$\end{document} to the output Schur mode q2 := v2. Thus, Q = [q2, q1] and


T=(λ2ω0λ1)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle T=\left(\begin{array}{cc}\lambda_{2} & \omega \\ 0 & \lambda_{1}\end{array}\right)$$\end{document}

A matrix A ∈ ℝ2 with complex eigenvalues λr ± iλω can be written in real Schur form as A = QT Q⊺ where


T=(λrabλr)=(λrϵλω−λω/ϵλr)
